HYPOTHESIS (data-grounded, NLSY79, n=12,686)

YES — higher time preference (greater present-orientation) is associated with higher BMI.

Operationalization:
- Time preference (exposure, 1989; the only wave with explicit savings behavior) = household
  savings direction: put more money INTO savings (low time preference) vs TOOK money OUT of
  savings or NO SAVINGS (high time preference / present-oriented), using
  "Was more money put into or taken out of R/spouse savings since last interview, 1989"
  (1=PUT MORE IN, 2=TOOK MORE OUT, 3=NO CHANGE, 4=NO SAVINGS), corroborated by
  "Net amount put into savings, 1989" and "Net amount taken out of savings, 1989".

- Outcome = BMI at 1992 (after the 1989 exposure for temporal precedence), computed from
  "Weight of respondent, 1992" (empirically POUNDS, not the codebook's kg; BMI=703*lb/in^2)
  and adult height = "Height of respondent, 1985" (inches) with fallback to "Height of
  respondent, 1981" (decoded feet*100+inches, e.g. 507 -> 5'7" = 67 in).

Primary confounder-adjusted model (OLS, n=3,311 complete cases, SEs clustered by Sample ID):
  BMI(1992) ~ time preference + age + sex + race/ethnicity + family size + mother's/father's
  education + own education (highest grade 1979) + ASVAB cognitive composite + family income
  (1979/1985/1989) + family net wealth (1985/1990) + incarceration (ever sentenced).

- Headline binary result (high TP = took money out OR no savings): +0.54 BMI units
  (95% CI 0.15 to 0.94; p=0.007).
- Categorical gradient vs put-money-in reference: TOOK MONEY OUT +0.50 [0.16,0.84; p=0.004];
  NO CHANGE +0.32 [-0.25,0.89; ns]; NO SAVINGS +0.96 [0.31,1.61; p=0.004]. Monotone
  present-orientation gradient consistent with the unadjusted pattern
  (BMI means: put-in 25.4 < took-out 26.2 ≈ unchanged 26.2 < no-savings 26.7).

Robustness (all consistent in sign/direction unless noted):
- R1 savers vs drawers only: +0.51 [0.11,0.91]; R2 log(net savings): -0.030 per log-unit
  (more saving -> LOWER BMI, p=0.001) — mirrors the headline in reverse.
- R3 same-wave BMI 1989: +0.42 [-0.02,0.85; p=0.059]; R4 BMI change 1989->1992: -0.06
  [ns] — high TP associates with HIGHER BMI LEVELS but NOT with faster 1989-92 growth.
- R5 implausible-BMI exclusion (BMI in 14-60): unchanged (took-out +0.50, no-savings +0.98).
- R6 median-imputed covariates, n=8,503: took-out +0.43 [0.22,0.63], no-savings +0.52
  [0.22,0.82], both p<0.001.
- R7 sex subgroups: women +0.82 [0.11,1.53; p=0.024]; men +0.27 [-0.07,0.61; ns] —
  association concentrated among women.
- R8 + father's occupation-at-14 controls: +0.55 [0.16,0.93].
- R9 wealth-accumulation proxy (1985->1996) in place of savings: -0.005 [ns] — the
  alternative proxy alone does not replicate the effect (caveat).
- R10 BMI 1981 (measured BEFORE the 1989 savings exposure): +0.23 [-0.01,0.46; p=0.055] —
  a pre-existing gradient exists, suggesting a stable trait/selection component rather than
  a purely causal within-period effect.

Conclusion: the data support the hypothesis that higher time preference (proxied by drawing
down or not holding savings in 1989) predicts moderately higher BMI by 1992 after controlling
for demographics, family background, education, cognitive ability, income, wealth, and
incarceration. Effect ~ +0.5-0.6 BMI units (about 0.1 SD; equivalent to roughly 3-4 lb for an
average-height adult). Interpretation is observational; "lead to" reflects temporal precedence
(1989 -> 1992), not proven causality.

Residual limitations: savings is an assumed proxy for an unmeasured discounting trait; no 1981
time-preference measure (weak reverse/stable-trait signal in R10); non-random attrition by 1992
(30% missing weight) and 31% income non-response; effect is null in men and for BMI growth.
